<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#sarissa">Glossary</a> · Term · weapon</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sarissa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The sarissa was a very long pike, several metres in length, carried by the infantry of the Macedonian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a>. Introduced under Philip II, it allowed several ranks of soldiers to present their points to the enemy at once. It was central to the conquests of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire</a> and remained the core weapon of Hellenistic armies such as that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sarissa.jpg" alt="Sarissa" width="320" height="137" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: F. Mitchell, United States Military Academy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Makedonische_phalanx.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A sarissa had two main parts, a long wooden shaft and a metal tip; some may also have had a metal butt at the other end, with which the weapon could be stood in the ground without damage to the shaft. Ash, which is long, flexible and light, and cornel wood, which is shorter but stronger, are the likeliest woods. It replaced the considerably shorter dory.</p><p>Ancient writers agree that its length set it apart, but not on the figure: Theophrastus, a contemporary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, puts the longest at 12 cubits, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> gives 16 cubits in the original design, reduced in practice to 14. In modern units this suggests about 5.8 m in Alexander's time and perhaps 6.3 m later.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The weapon needed both hands, so the phalangite carried only a smaller shield, the pelta, perhaps hung from a neck strap. On the march the sarissas were held upright; levelled for battle, fully five rows of points projected in front of the first rank, while the rear ranks held theirs angled upward, which also deflected arrows. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> so formed was considered practically invulnerable from the front, but it turned with difficulty, and a soldier out of formation had to fall back on a sword or dagger.</p><p>Sarissa-armed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanxes</a> were vital in the early battles of Alexander, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gaugamela">Battle of Gaugamela</a>, though in his later campaigns he gradually reduced their importance. The surest way to beat such a phalanx was to break its formation or outflank it, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> did at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cynoscephalae">Battle of Cynoscephalae</a> in 197 BCE. A light cavalry unit of Alexander's army, the sarissophoroi, carried somewhat shorter versions; Nicholas Victor Sekunda argues that the Macedonian cavalry spear is better classed as a xyston.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>A leaf-shaped spearhead 51 cm long, found by Manolis Andronikos in a Macedonian tomb at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aigai">Vergina</a>, shaped much scholarship between 1970 and 2000, but more recent work doubts that it belonged to a sarissa; Sekunda favours a small diamond-shaped iron tip of about 13.5 cm. Earlier estimates put the weight at 5.5 to 6.5 kg, yet Peter Connolly reconstructed a pike 5.8 m long in cherry wood that weighed only 4.05 kg. Scholars also draw on later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pike">pikemen</a>, such as the Swiss and the German Landsknecht: preserved English pikes are 5.6 to 5.9 m long, which suggests that the lengths reported for the Macedonian weapon were workable.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sarissa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The core of Antigonid power was the Macedonian phalanx, a dense formation of infantry armed with the <b>sarissa</b>, a pike some five to six metres long, supported by peltasts, cavalry and mercenaries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>He introduced the Macedonian phalanx armed with the long <b>sarissa</b> pike, gave the infantry peltai shields, helmets, greaves and cuirasses or kotthybos stomach bands, formed the elite hypaspistai and the royal bodyguards, the somatophylakes, adopted the Scythian flying-wedge cavalry formation, hired Cretan archers and Thracian, Paeonian and Illyrian…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-macedon">Kingdom of Macedon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Philip II's phalanx carried the <b>sarissa</b>, a pike of up to about six metres, letting several ranks present their points to the enemy at once.</blockquote><a href="/empire/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The Seleucid army was built around the Macedonian-style phalanx, dense ranks of infantry armed with the <b>sarissa</b>, a pike some five to seven metres long that presented an almost impenetrable wall of points to any frontal assault.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
